L1: Effects of a beetroot juice with high neobetanin content on the early-phase
L2: insulin response in healthy volunteers
L3: Journal of Nutritional Science (2014)
L4: Peter C. Wootton-Beard, Kirsten Brandt, David Fell, Sarah Warner, Lisa Ryan
L5: 1 Functional Food Centre ,
L6:  Oxford Brookes University ,  Gipsy Lane, Oxford OX3
L7: 0BP ,  UK
L8: 2 Human Nutrition Research Centre, School of
L9: Agriculture and Rural Development ,  Newcastle
L10: University ,  Newcastle upon Tyne NE1 7RU ,
L11:  UK
L12: 3 Cell Systems Modelling Group ,
L13:  Oxford Brookes University ,  Gipsy Lane, Oxford OX3
L14: 0BP ,  UK
L15: 4 Department of Nutrition and Dietetics ,
L16:  Monash University ,  Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and
L17: Health Sciences, 264 Ferntree Gully Road, Vic 3168 ,
L18:  Australia
L19: Produce rich in phytochemicals may alter postprandial glucose and insulin responses by
L20: interacting with the pathways that regulate glucose uptake and insulin secretion in
L21: humans. The aims of the present study were to assess the phytochemical constituents of red
L22: beetroot juice and to measure the postprandial glucose and insulin responses elicited by
L23: either 225 ml beetroot juice (BEET), a control beverage matched for macronutrient content
L24: (MCON) or a glucose beverage in healthy adults. Beetroot juice was a particularly rich
L25: source of betalain degradation compounds. The orange/yellow pigment neobetanin was
L26: measured in particularly high quantities (providing 1·3 g in the 225 ml). A total of
L27: sixteen healthy individuals were recruited, and consumed the test meals in a controlled
L28: single-blind cross-over design. Results revealed a significant lowering of the
L29: postprandial insulin response in the early phase (0–60 min)
L30: ( P  < 0·05) and a significantly lower glucose response in the
L31: 0–30 min phase ( P  < 0·05) in the BEET treatment compared with
L32: MCON. Betalains, polyphenols and dietary nitrate found in the beetroot juice may each
L33: contribute to the observed differences in the postprandial insulin concentration.
L34: Metabolism and Metabolic Studies
L35: Postprandial glycaemia; Phytochemicals; Betalains; BEET, beetroot beverage; CHO, carbohydrate; GLUC, glucose beverage; iAUC, incremental AUC; MCON, matched control beverage; sAUC, segmental AUC
L36: Experimental methods
L37: Determination of nitrate, nitrite, betalain and phenolic content of the beetroot
L38: juice
L39: Nitrate and nitrite concentrations were measured in duplicate in samples from six
L40: separate bottles of Beet It® organic beetroot juice with lemon by GC-MS as isotope
L41: dilution after derivatisation with 2,3,4,5,6-pentafluorobenzyl bromide (PFB-Br) according
L42: to Tsikas ( 
L43:  18 
L44:  )  with minor modifications. Briefly, known concentrations of
L45: [ 15 N]nitrate and [ 15 N]nitrite as internal standards were added to
L46: the beetroot juice. Then 200 µl spiked sample, 800 µl acetone and 20 µl PFB-Br were mixed
L47: and incubated for 20 min at 50°C in a sealed tube, after which the acetone was evaporated
L48: under an N 2  stream. A quantity of 2 ml toluene and 1 ml water was added and
L49: shaken for 1 min. After phase separation, the upper (toluene) phase was analysed on a
L50: GC-MS (Shimadzu Corporation) with an Optima 17 column (15 m, 0·25 mm internal diameter,
L51: 0·25 µm film thickness), using negative-ion chemical ionisation, splitless mode, He
L52: (70 kPa) as column carrier, methane (200 Pa) as reagent. Initial column temperature was
L53: 70°C, held for 1 min, then increased 30°C/min to 280°C, with electron energy 230 eV and
L54: electron current 300 µA, ion source 180°C, interface 280°C and injector 200°C.
L55: [ 15 N] and [ 14 N]nitrate were measured at
L56:  m / z  63 and 62, respectively, with retention time (RT)
L57: 3·2 min, [ 15 N] and [ 14 N]nitrite at
L58:  m / z  47 and 46, respectively, with RT 3·4 min.
L59: Betalains and phenolic compounds were measured using the method of Nemzer  et
L60: al. ( 
L61:  19 
L62:  )  on a Shimadzu Prominence HPLC with a Luna C-18 analytical column
L63: (25 cm, 3 mm internal diameter, 5 µm particle size; Phenomenex) and an SPD-M20A diode
L64: array detector (Shimadzu). Solvents were B, acetonitrile; and A, 2 % formic acid in water.
L65: The gradient was 3 % B at 0 min, 16 % B at 17 min, 50 % B at 30 min, 100 % B at 32–35 min
L66: and 3 % B at 35–45 min with 0·5 ml/min flow rate, 35°C column temperature and 10 µl
L67: injection volume. Peaks were recorded at 538 nm (betanins), 480 nm (betaxanthins and
L68: neobetanin), 505 nm (decarboxylated betanins) and 320 nm (phenolic compounds). Phenolic
L69: compounds were quantified at 320 nm rather than at the maximum wavelength for each
L70: compound; this was for practical reasons since most of them were not specifically
L71: identified, and this is a commonly used wavelength to measure phenolic compounds.
L72: Quantification of betanin, isobetanin, 17-decarboxyisobetanin and neobetanin was done by
L73: comparison with standards isolated by semi-preparative HPLC from the beetroot juice using
L74: the same equipment and solvents, on a Develosil ODS-HG-5 HPLC column (RP-18, 250 × 20 mm
L75: internal diameter), 5 µm particle size. The gradient was 3 % B at 0 min, 16 % B at 20 min,
L76: 50 % B at 40 min, 100 % B at 47 min until 60 min, with 3 ml/min flow rate, 35°C column
L77: temperature and 400 µl injection volume. Quantification of flavonoids and phenolic acids
L78: was done by comparison with authentic standards of rutin and chlorogenic acid,
L79: respectively, measured at 320 nm. In all, four peaks had UV spectra that matched typical
L80: flavonoid spectra and eighteen peaks were similar to phenolic acids.
L81: Subjects and study design
L82: A group of sixteen healthy adults (six male and ten female) were recruited by poster for
L83: the present study. Subject characteristics are provided in  Table 1 . The present study was conducted according to the guidelines
L84: laid down in the Declaration of Helsinki and all procedures involving human
L85: subjects/patients were approved by the Oxford Brookes University Ethics Committee
L86: (110553). Written informed consent was obtained from all subjects. Participants were asked
L87: to visit the laboratory on three separate occasions. Before the first visit each
L88: participant was asked to complete a confidential health questionnaire pertaining to their
L89: medical history, a habitual physical activity questionnaire, and a modified FFQ designed
L90: to provide an estimate of their habitual polyphenol intake. Each visit was separated by no
L91: less than 48 h. At the start of each visit anthropometric measurements were taken and
L92: adherence to a 12 h fast, abstinence from caffeine, alcohol and intense physical activity
L93: as well as adequate hydration were assured verbally. Participants were excluded if they
L94: had type 2 diabetes or a fasting blood glucose measure of >6·1 mmol/l. Participants
L95: provided two baseline finger capillary samples separated by 5 min before consuming the
L96: test beverage. In a randomised, single-blind, cross-over design, three test beverages were
L97: provided to each subject.  Table 1. Participant characteristics (Mean values and standard deviations) Mean 
L98:  sd 
L99:  Age (years) 27 5 Height (cm) 172·4 8·6 Weight (kg) 69·2 11·7 BMI (kg/m 2 ) 23·3 2·8 Fat (%) 23·0 7·0 Physical activity index  Work 2·44 0·25  Sport 3·71 2·06  Leisure 3·19 0·49 Polyphenol intake (g/d) 1·93 0·59
L100: Study protocol
L101: The first beverage (BEET) consisted of 225 ml Beet It® organic beetroot juice with lemon
L102: (<2 %) (James White Drinks Ltd) providing a total of 50 g available CHO. The second
L103: beverage (MCON) consisted of a matched control drink (225 ml) containing sucrose,
L104: fructose, glucose, pea protein isolate, inulin and sodium chloride providing a total of
L105: 50 g available CHO (the precise composition of BEET and MCON are shown in  Table 2 ). The third beverage (GLUC) contained 50 g
L106: available CHO as glucose (225 ml). On each test day postprandial finger-prick blood
L107: samples were taken at 5, 15, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120 and 150 min (time points T5, T15, T30,
L108: T45, T60, T90, T120 and T150, respectively) to measure both blood glucose (5 µl) and
L109: plasma insulin (300 µl) using a Unistik 3 single-use lancing device (Owen Mumford).
L110: Glucose concentration was measured using a Hemocue Glucose 201+ analyser (HemoCue Limited)
L111: calibrated to plasma equivalent glucose concentrations. For each meal the incremental AUC
L112: (iAUC) was calculated geometrically ignoring the area below the baseline ( 
L113:  20 
L114:  ) . Plasma samples were analysed for insulin using a radioimmunoassay on
L115: an automated immunoanalyser (Cobas E411; Roche Diagnostics). Plasma was separated from
L116: whole blood by centrifugation (4000 rpm for 10 min) and stored at –40°C for no more than 1
L117: month. The procedures used to measure the glucose and insulin responses were adapted from
L118: those described by Brouns  et al. ( 
L119:  21 
L120:  )  and Wolever ( 
L121:  22 
L122:  )  and were in line with the recommendations of the
L123: FAO/WHO ( 
L124:  20 
L125:  ) .  Table 2. Nutrient composition of the beetroot juice beverage (BEET), matched control
L126: beverage (MCON) and glucose control beverage (GLUC) Nutrient BEET (per 225 ml)* MCON (per 225 ml) GLUC (per 225 ml) Energy  kJ 931 943 941  kcal 223 225 225 Reduced N (g) 0·91 0·91 – Fat (g) 0·00 0·08 – Carbohydrate (g) 50·4 50·4 50·0  Of which sucrose (g) 39·35 39·35 –  Of which glucose (g) 4·77 4·77 50·0  Of which fructose (g) 5·13 5·13 – Fibre 1·13 1·13 – Na (mg) 117 117 – * Data from Eurofins Testing Ltd (unpublished results) on the carbohydrate
L127: composition of Beet It® organic beetroot juice with lemon.
L128: FFQ were analysed using the Phenol-Explorer database ( 
L129:  23 
L130:  )  to estimate habitual polyphenol intake. The Phenol-Explorer database
L131: lists food polyphenol content according to a number of common methods. In this instance
L132: the values obtained by the Folin–Ciocalteu method were used, as these are available for
L133: the highest number of foods, allowing for the best representation of habitual intake for
L134: this type of enquiry. Habitual polyphenol intake (g/d) is displayed in  Table 1 . Habitual physical activity was assessed
L135: using the physical activity index proposed by Baecke  et
L136: al. ( 
L137:  24 
L138:  ) . Based on a scale, proportionate to the levels of physical exertion
L139: required to perform the activity, each question in a standard questionnaire is given a
L140: value between 1 and 5, with the exception of sport and exercise which is calculated as a
L141: product of the average energy used for the activity (MJ), intensity of performance and the
L142: time spent performing. Physical activity is displayed in  Table 1 .
L143: Insulin sensitivity
L144: An index of insulin sensitivity (S I ) was calculated using the oral glucose
L145: minimal model reported by Burattini  et al. ( 
L146:  25 
L147:  )  in their investigation of insulin action and secretion that used a
L148: model based on the oral glucose tolerance test. It is an amalgamation of the classic
L149: minimal model of glucose kinetics coupled with an equation describing the rate of
L150: appearance of glucose into the circulation ( 
L151:  26 
L152:  ) . The following equation describes the model: where AUC denotes the AUC of the quantities in parentheses during the total
L153: time course ( t ) 0–( t ) 150, and ΔG( t )
L154: and ΔI( t ) are glycaemia and insulinaemia above fasting values. GE is
L155: glucose effectiveness and f is the fraction of glucose appearing in the systemic
L156: circulation; values of 3·7 × 10 −2  min/dl per kg and 0·87, respectively, are
L157: used in accordance with those reported by Burattini  et
L158: al. ( 
L159:  25 
L160:  ) .   is the dose of glucose per kg body weight and is calculated individually
L161: for each subject.
L162: Statistical analyses
L163: A mixed-model ANOVA with two factors (‘meal’ and ‘time’) was conducted but the assumed
L164: covariance matrix was discovered to be significantly different from the observed matrix.
L165: Therefore, comparisons of the data were made by repeated-measures ANOVA for glucose and
L166: insulin at each time point. Main effects were compared for the within-subjects factor
L167: ‘meal’ and corrected with a Bonferroni adjustment. Both iAUC and segmental AUC (sAUC) were
L168: also calculated and compared using repeated-measures ANOVA. Statistical power was
L169: calculated in consultation with the statistics department at Oxford Brookes University and
L170: is based on the mean and standard deviation of pilot data using similar conditions and
L171: participants. A minimum detectable difference of 10 % was chosen for insulin, as the
L172: spread of data from T0 to T150 is quite large and insulin is the primary outcome measure.
L173: Power was calculated using an online calculator for cross-over studies whereby the outcome
L174: is a measurement. The results showed that a power of 0·9 would be achieved at a
L175: significance level of  P  < 0·05 using sixteen participants.
L176: Results
L177: Phytochemical composition
L178: Average nitrate concentration was 4·40 g/l, ranging from 3·03 to 5·24 g/l in six
L179: individual bottles, with an average relative  sd  within samples of 2·5 %. Average
L180: nitrite concentration was 2·34 mg/l, ranging from 2·23 to 2·55 mg/l in individual bottles,
L181: with an average relative standard deviation within samples of 2·0 %. There was no
L182: correlation between nitrate and nitrite concentrations when comparing individual bottles
L183: ( R 
L184:  2 
L185:   0·147;  P  = 0·781). Values for the sum of betalains, betaxanthins,
L186: flavonoids, phenolic acids and some individual betalains are shown in  Table 3 . The beetroot juice was a particularly rich
L187: source of the orange/yellow pigment neobetanin (1263 mg per 225 ml) and contained a total
L188: of 129 mg polyphenolic compounds per 225 ml. Analysis of the CHO content of the beetroot
L189: juice showed that the composition was 90 % sucrose, 5 % glucose, 4 % fructose with minor
L190: amounts of maltose and lactose (<0·1 g per 100 g). Crude protein content was 2·53 g
L191: per 100 g, and total fibre was <0·5 g per 100 g. The value for neobetanin was
L192: adjusted for a low recovery (47·5 %) measured by quantification of a purified sample with
L193: analytical HPLC and compared with the spectrophotometric value. The recovery was even less
L194: during semi-preparative HPLC.  Table 3. Phytochemical composition of the beetroot juice beverage (Mean values with their standard errors ( n  6), and relative
