L245: iAUC analysis revealed no significant difference between the three conditions for insulin
L246: response ( Table 4 ). The data were separated into
L247: the 0–30, 0–45, 0–60 and 0–90 min segments and further analysed ( Fig. 2 ). The BEET condition elicited a significantly lower insulin
L248: response in the 0–30, 0–45 and 0–60 min segments ( P  < 0·05 for
L249: each) compared with MCON. The data were also analysed by time point ( Table 4 ). A non-significant, lower insulin response
L250: was observed for BEET compared with MCON at T5 ( P  = 0·08), which reached
L251: significance at T15 ( P  < 0·05) and remained evident, although not
L252: significant, at T30 ( P  = 0·09). The BEET condition was shown to elicit a
L253: significantly higher insulin response than MCON at T120
L254: ( P  < 0·05), although the actual values are relatively small.  Fig. 2. Segmental areas under the blood insulin response curve (sAUC). Values are means
