L261: Physical activity and polyphenol consumption
L262: The mean physical activity index for this cohort was 3·11 ( sd  0·75).
L263: Individually, the work index was 2·44 ( sd  0·25), the sport index was 3·71
L264: ( sd  2·06) and the leisure index was 3·19 ( sd  0·49). These values
L265: indicate a recreationally active cohort. Polyphenol intake for this cohort was 1925
L266: ( sd  592) mg/d with a range 853–2807 mg/d. This suggests a cohort with a
L267: relatively high habitual intake of polyphenols.
L268: Insulin sensitivity
L269: Insulin sensitivity was calculated according the minimal model proposed by Caumo
L270:  et al. ( 
L271:  26 
L272:  ) . The data were log transformed to reduce the impact of high
L273: inter-individual variability in insulin response. No significant differences were observed
L274: between the conditions. Insulin sensitivity was 1·05 × 10 −5  dl/kg per min per
L275: pmol/l (95 % CI 0·55, 2·01) for MCON compared with 1·68 × 10 −5  dl/kg per min
L276: per pmol/l (95 % CI 0·86, 3·29) for the BEET condition. Insulin sensitivity in the GLUC
L277: condition was 2·10 × 10 −5  dl/kg per min per pmol/l (95 % CI 1·08, 4·06).
L278: Discussion
L279: The aim of the present study was to investigate the effects of a beetroot juice on
L280: postprandial glucose and insulin concentrations as well as insulin sensitivity and
L281: characterise the phytochemicals of this juice. There are two possible confounders of these
L282: results that are hereby considered. Acids have been shown to reduce postprandial glucose and
L283: insulin concentrations ( 
L284:  27 
L285:  , 
L286:  28 
L287:  ) . There is a small amount of lemon juice present in the beetroot beverage
L288: (<2 %); Leeman  et al. ( 
L289:  27 
L290:  )  showed that acids were required in gram quantities to observe a
L291: significant reduction in postprandial glycaemia. The possible confounding of these results
L292: by the lemon juice in this beverage may therefore be considered to be negligible. Beetroot
L293: juice contains a number of bioactive components including betaxanthins, betanins,
L294: flavonoids, phenolic acids ( 
L295:  15 
L296:  )  and betanin degradation products produced by thermal processing ( Table 3 ). The concentration of betaxanthin was
L297: relatively low (3·29 mg per 225 ml), as was the concentration of intact betanins (48·4 mg
L298: per 225 ml). The betanins were divided into 18·8 mg betanin and 29·5 mg isobetanin. Both
L299: flavonoids and phenolic acids were present in moderate concentrations (72·7 mg per 225 ml
L300: and 55·9 mg per 225 ml, respectively). The most abundant compounds in the beetroot juice
L301: were betanin degradation products formed, presumably, by thermal processing techniques.
L302: These were divided into two predominant compounds: 17-decarboxy-isobetanin (5·08 mg per
L303: 225 ml); and the orange/yellow pigment; neobetanin (1264 mg per 225 ml). The much higher CV
L304: (among bottles) for the betalain pigments than for the non-betalain types of phenolic
L305: compounds indicates that some of the pigment degradation took place after the juice was
L306: distributed into the containers, while the non-betalain compounds did not degrade. The
L307: contents of nitrate and nitrite were within the ranges previously found for processed
L308: beetroot products ( 
L309:  29 
L310:  ) . The nitrite levels were relatively low, indicating that little or no
L311: bacterial denitrification had occurred during processing and storage.
L312: There was a similar glycaemic response across the three conditions with the exception of a
L313: significantly lower glycaemic response at 5 min ( P  < 0·05), which
L314: persisted, although not significantly, across the proceeding minutes, sufficient to cause a
L315: significantly lower glycaemic response ( P  < 0·05) to be detected in
L316: the 0–30 min sAUC for BEET compared with MCON. The moderately attenuated glycaemic response
L317: was accompanied by a similarly attenuated early-phase insulin response. A significantly
L318: lower insulin response was detected at 15 min for BEET compared with MCON, with further
L319: lower responses measured at 5 and 30 min that did not reach significance
L320: ( P  = 0·08 and  P  = 0·09, respectively). These differences
L321: were sufficient to cause significant differences to also be observed in the sAUC segments
L322: between 0–30, 0–45 and 0–60 min for BEET compared with MCON. These results suggest, although
L323: not conclusively, that bioactive compounds within beetroot juice may have the potential to
L324: help control postprandial glycaemia. Participants were asked to refrain from unusual eating
L325: habits, but some participants may still have consumed large quantities of phytochemicals or
L326: particularly high quantities of CHO with their evening meal. This is unlikely to have had a
L327: significant impact upon the results since the half-life of polyphenols and betalains is
L328: reported to be approximately 5 h in the blood ( 
L329:  30 
L330:  , 
L331:  31 
L332:  ) .
L333: Törrönen  et al. ( 
L334:  32 
L335:  )  fed fourteen healthy participants a blackcurrant juice beverage
L336: containing 50 g sucrose fortified with crowberry powder, which doubled the polyphenol
L337: content compared with the placebo beverage. The crowberry powder was a particularly rich
L338: source of anthocyanins, a group within the flavonoid family, and the test beverage provided
L339: 293 mg polyphenols per 100 ml. The postprandial glycaemic response in the study by Törrönen
L340:  et al. ( 
L341:  32 
L342:  )  followed a similar pattern to the present study with no significant
L343: differences overall, but a larger (although not significant) glucose and insulin response
L344: observed in the early phase (up to 30 min) for the control condition compared with the test
L345: condition. Törrönen  et al. ( 
L346:  32 
L347:  )  assigned their results to the higher polyphenol content of the test
L348: juice, and suggested reduced digestion of sucrose and/or a slower release of glucose in the
L349: gut as the likely mechanism(s). The addition of berries, rich in polyphenols, to a sucrose
L350: load has previously been shown to attenuate the postprandial glucose and insulin responses
L351: in adults ( 
L352:  33 
L353:  – 
L354:  35 
L355:  ) . A number of other studies have also investigated the effect of
L356: polyphenol-rich interventions on glucose and/or insulin response in healthy subjects.
L357: Overall, a high proportion of these studies show a delayed rise in glucose and/or insulin in
L358: the early phase of the postprandial response, which reaches significance in some but not all
L359: studies. These studies are summarised in  Table
L360: 5 ( 
L361:  7 
L362:  , 
L363:  32 
L364:  – 
L365:  45 
L366:  ) . Some studies have analysed blood taken from venous samples whilst
L367: others (including the present study) have utilised capillary sampling. No difference has
L368: been observed between capillary and venous sampling, although capillary sampling has
L369: previously been suggested to be more accurate ( 
L370:  21 
