L428: Previous studies have reported polyphenol intakes of 300–800 mg as sufficient to attenuate
L429: postprandial glycaemia when consumed alongside CHO ( 
L430:  32 
L431:  , 
L432:  34 
L433:  ) . The beetroot juice beverage in the present study provided a total of
L434: 129 mg of polyphenols together with 1393 mg of betanins and betanin degradation products, in
L435: particular 1263 mg neobetanin. The present study thus suggests that betanins (particularly
L436: the degradation product neobetanin) and/or nitrate may have comparable effects to phenolic
L437: compounds, since the content of flavonoids and phenolic acids contained in beetroot juice is
L438: too low to fully account for the observed effects. Neobetanin was identified in the 1980s as
L439: the orange/yellow pigment 5- O -β- d -glucopyranosylneobetanidin
L440: isolated from red beetroot ( 
L441:  46 
L442:  – 
L443:  48 
L444:  ) .
L445: As has been discussed previously, the potential beneficial effects of phytochemicals on the
L446: control of postprandial glycaemia may be due to a reduced digestion of glucose, reduced
L447: absorption of glucose, stimulation of insulin release or alterations in insulin
L448: signalling/sensitivity. The reduced glucose response in the 0–30 min period following
L449: consumption of the beetroot beverage and the persistently reduced insulin response over the
L450: first 60 min also observed following ingestion of the beetroot beverage in the present study
L451: suggest that either glucose uptake is blunted, resulting in reduced insulin secretion, or
L452: that less insulin is required to restore glucose homeostasis resulting in a reduction in
L453: circulating insulin. There is not sufficient evidence presented in the present study to
L454: eliminate any potential mechanism, nor are the differences observed in the responses
L455: sufficient to confirm any of the potential mechanisms, although there is a suggested
L456: increase in insulin sensitivity. The insulin sensitivity model that was applied to the data
L457: did not reveal any significant differences between the three conditions, which may be
L458: largely due to the apparent inter-individual variability of human glycaemic response data.
L459: The beetroot condition did result in a higher insulin sensitivity score (1·68 (95 % CI 0·86,
L460: 3·29) × 10 −5  dl/kg per min per pmol/l) than the matched control condition (1·05
L461: (95 % CI 0·55, 2·01) × 10 −5  dl/kg per min per pmol/l), which did not approach
L462: significance ( P  = 0·24).
L463: The high concentration of betalains found in the beetroot juice, particularly betanin
L464: degradation products such as neobetanin, suggests that they are the most likely compounds to
L465: be responsible for any of the alterations observed. Betalains bear certain structural
L466: similarities to the anthocyanin group of polyphenolic compounds, a group which has also been
L467: shown to alter postprandial glycaemia ( 
L468:  32 
L469:  ) . Each species of plants contains either betalains or anthocyanins, since
L470: the two groups of compounds play exactly the same role in the plant ( 
L471:  49 
L472:  ) . It would therefore not be surprising if these compounds also have
L473: similar effects on mammalian physiology.
L474: A review of ninety-seven bioavailability studies showed that ingested polyphenolic
L475: compounds reach the low micromolar range in plasma ( 
L476:  30 
L477:  ) . These concentrations were observed for flavanols and catechins, whilst
L478: anthocyanins reached only high nanomolar concentrations. There are no studies to date
L479: assessing the bioavailability or metabolism of betalain degradation products in humans.
L480: Tesoriere  et al. ( 
L481:  50 
L482:  )  examined the absorption, excretion and distribution of native betalains
L483: from cactus pear fruit in human LDL. Tesoriere  et al. ( 
L484:  50 
L485:  )  showed that betanin contained in cactus pear fruit reached a maximum
L486: plasma concentration of 0·2 µmol/l after approximately 3 h following an intake of 16 mg,
L487: which indicates that betalains reach the plasma in relatively high concentrations compared
L488: with anthocyanins. Betalains have a short half-life in the plasma and erythrocytes (about
L489: 5 h) ( 
L490:  31 
L491:  ) , which may limit their potential function to processes lasting only a
L492: short period such as glucose digestion and absorption. For this potential effect to be
L493: prolonged, the desired compounds would need to be ingested in sufficient quantity, at each
L494: eating episode containing CHO. The effect of nitrate in this phenomenon cannot be ruled out
L495: since polyphenols can mimic nitrate by enhancing the endogenous production of
L496: NO ( 
L497:  51 
L498:  ) . However, the opposite effect of nitrate mimicking polyphenols has yet
L499: to be studied.
L500: Conclusions
L501: This is the first study to investigate the effects of beetroot juice on postprandial
L502: glycaemia. The study showed that beetroot juice elicited a significant suppression of
L503: postprandial glycaemia in the 0–30 min sAUC ( P  < 0·05) and
L504: postprandial insulinaemia in the 0–30, 0–45 and 0–60 sAUC
L505: ( P  < 0·05) when compared with a control beverage matched for CHO
L506: content, which was comparable with that observed using berries in other investigations.
L507: HPLC analysis of the beetroot juice revealed that the predominant secondary metabolite
L508: (excluding nitrate) in this product is the yellow/orange pigment neobetanin, derived from
L509: betanin, as well as significant, but much smaller, amounts of other betalains and
L510: polyphenolic compounds. Neobetanin's size and chemical properties (for example, conjugated
L511: system) are similar to those of flavonoid polyphenolics such as anthocyanins and it is
L512: suggested, therefore, that neobetanin probably contributes to the observed effect, which
L513: is similar to effects previously reported for polyphenolic compounds. Insulin sensitivity
L514: models applied to the data suggested that insulin sensitivity in the tested cohorts was
L515: non-significantly increased with beetroot juice compared with control.
L516: The potential for bioactive phytochemicals to modify substrate metabolism in the
L517: postprandial state is a developing area of interest. Reports by Murase  et
L518: al. ( 
L519:  52 
L520:  )  on coffee polyphenols (caffeic acid derivatives), Tiwari  et
L521: al. ( 
L522:  53 
L523:  )  on the effects of Indian vegetable juices and Linderborg  et
L524: al. ( 
L525:  44 
L526:  )  on sea buckthorn have all demonstrated significant effects on glucose
L527: and insulin responses. Hyperglycaemia, hyperinsulinaemia and hyperlipidaemia are crucial
L528: in the development of CVD, type 2 diabetes and the metabolic syndrome, and whether or not
L529: they are able to be modified by dietary components is of significant ongoing interest.
